Rum baba
6
Yeasted babas soaked in rum and spice syrup, topped with Chantilly cream.

---
Baba dough
wheat flour|250|g
fine salt|0.5|g
powdered sugar|15|g
water|50|ml
fresh yeast|15|g
eggs|175|g
soft butter|75|g

Chantilly cream
double cream|250|ml
powdered sugar|5|g

Rum syrup
water|1000|ml
powdered sugar|500|g
vanilla pod|1|pcs
star anise|1|pcs
cinnamon stick|1|pcs
unwaxed orange (zest)|240|g
dark rum|30|ml

---
Preheat the oven to 200°C.
In the bowl of a mixer, mix the sugar and salt dissolved in a little water.
Dissolve the yeast in the rest of the warm water and add to the mixture.
Add the flour and knead gently.
Add the eggs in 2 or 3 goes and knead about 7 min until the dough is smooth.
Add the melted, lukewarm butter and knead until the dough is smooth and even.
Fill baba moulds three-quarters full and leave to rise at room temperature until the dough rises above the rim (about 1 hour).
Bake 20 to 30 min at 200°C, until evenly coloured.

Make the syrup: in a saucepan, put the water, sugar, orange zest and spices.
Bring to the boil to dissolve the sugar, then infuse for a few minutes.
Add the rum off the heat and mix well. Keep the syrup warm.

Turn out the babas while still warm and soak them in the warm syrup until well soaked.
Let them drain on a rack before topping.

Whip the very cold cream to soft peaks.
Add the powdered sugar at the end to stiffen.
Pipe a generous amount of Chantilly onto each baba.

---
The syrup should be warm so the babas absorb it well.
Adjust the amount of rum to taste and your guests.
Babas keep better in the fridge; add Chantilly just before serving.

---
dessert
baba
rum
viennoiserie
